<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sayajirao Gaekwad III</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1863">1863</a>–<a href="/years/1939">1939</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sayajirao Gaekwad III was maharaja of Baroda from 1875 to 1939, chosen from a distant branch of the family after the British deposed Malhar Rao. He made Baroda one of the most progressive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, introducing compulsory primary education, a public library network, an independent judiciary and railways, and banning child marriage. He funded scholars including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii.jpg" alt="Sayajirao Gaekwad III" width="320" height="441" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lafayette · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sayajirao_Gaekwad_III,_Maharaja_of_Baroda,_1919.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>He was born Gopalrao Gaekwad on 11 March 1863 in the village of Kaulane, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a> district, the second son of Kashirao Bhikajirao Gaekwad and Ummabai. His branch descended from a morganatic marriage of the first Raja of Baroda and was not expected to inherit. After the Resident Robert Phayre reported Malharrao's tyranny and Malharrao tried to poison him with arsenic, Lord Salisbury, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-of-state-for-india">Secretary of State for India</a>, had him deposed on 10 April 1875.</p><p>Maharani Jamnabai, widow of Khanderao Gaekwad, summoned the heads of the family's branches with their sons. Kashirao and his three sons walked some 600 kilometres to Baroda, where Gopalrao is reported to have said, &quot;I have come here to rule&quot;. The British selected him; Jamnabai adopted him on 27 May 1875, renaming him Sayajirao, and he took the throne on 16 June 1875.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Until he received full powers on 28 December 1881 a Council of Regency governed, and Raja Sir T. Madhava Rao tutored him in administration. His reforms included divorce legislation, the removal of untouchability, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> studies and the encouragement of the fine arts. In 1906 he introduced compulsory and free primary education, the first Indian ruler to do so, and in 1908 he founded the Bank of Baroda. A gravity water supply from Ajwa, planned in 1892, still serves much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vadodara">Vadodara</a>.</p><p>He clashed often with the Residents and the Viceroy, and openly supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-durbar">Delhi Durbar</a> of 1911 he bowed only once, perfunctorily, before King-Emperor George V and turned his back on him; the British never fully trusted him again, though he was awarded the GCIE in 1919. He brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-aurobindo">Sri Aurobindo</a> to Baroda College in 1893, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dadabhai-naoroji">Dadabhai Naoroji</a> his start as Dewan, and patronised the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raja-ravi-varma">Raja Ravi Varma</a> and the musicians of the Gayan Shala, founded in 1886 by Ustad Moula Bux.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Sayajirao died on 6 February 1939 after a reign of 63 years. His son Fatehsinhrao had died in 1908, and his grandson Pratap Singh Rao Gaekwad succeeded him. Funds he set aside at his Diamond Jubilee went to a university for rural students, completed as the Maharaja Sayajirao University, and the public park Sayaji Baug was his gift to the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sayajirao Gaekwad III” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His adopted successor <b>Sayajirao Gaekwad III</b> (1875–1939) introduced compulsory primary education, a state library network, courts and railways, banned child marriage and funded scholars including B. R. Ambedkar.</blockquote><a href="/state/baroda-state">Baroda State</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vadodara">Vadodara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vadodara">Vadodara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=22.3,73.2&amp;year=1912" aria-label="Vadodara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Sayajirao Gaekwad III"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6035 5597h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6035 5597h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=22.3,73.2&amp;year=1912">What was here in 1912? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
